<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<PermissionSet xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
      Everything the rlmDecisionTableManager component needs, and nothing else.

      Deliberately separate from RLM_UtilitiesPermset: that set also grants
      RLM_AccountUtilities, whose invocable deletes account-related orders,
      assets, contracts, invoices, quotes and opportunities. Assigning it just to
      let someone refresh a decision table would hand them destructive Apex
      access, so this component gets its own least-privilege set — the same
      reasoning that gave RLM_ExpressionSetManager its own.
    -->
    <classAccesses>
        <apexClass>RLM_DecisionTableManagerController</apexClass>
        <enabled>true</enabled>
    </classAccesses>
    <!--
      No objectPermissions, and that is the correct answer rather than an
      omission. Verified on a live org (2026-07-26, rlm-base__usagetest) against
      every object the controller's USER_MODE queries read:

      - DecisionTable, DecisionTableSourceCriteria and Organization hold ZERO
        ObjectPermissions rows org-wide, so object perms on them are silently
        dropped on deploy and their read is governed by platform/setup access,
        not perm-set object perms. Same class of platform entity as the
        ContextDefinition/ExpressionSetDefinition exception documented in
        RLM_ExpressionSetManager.
      - CalculationMatrix (read for the Lookup Type field) DOES model object
        perms, and read is already granted by the standard Revenue Cloud sets a
        build assigns anyway — RLM_PSL, RLM_NGP, RLM_PCM, RLM_USG,
        RLM_Product_and_Pricing_Admin, RLM_Sales_Operations and the
        UnifiedCatalog PSLs. Re-granting it here would duplicate an existing
        grant for no gain, and an over-grant is its own audit finding.

      No fieldPermissions either: every field these queries select is
      permissionable=false, so object access covers them. Declaring them would
      fail to deploy or over-grant.

      If CalculationMatrix read is ever missing, the component degrades honestly
      — loadLookupTypes swallows the failure and the Lookup Type row is omitted
      from the details pane rather than showing a wrong value.
    -->
    <hasActivationRequired>false</hasActivationRequired>
    <label>RLM Decision Table Manager</label>
</PermissionSet>
